# Docker runtime

- `internal/docker.Client` is the only code that runs `docker`. It takes an injectable `Runner` (real: `ExecRunner`; tests: `internal/docker/dockertest.Fake`, an in-memory CLI that records argv, child env and env-file content, inherits image labels on `create` and parses env files like the real CLI). No shell, a timeout on every call (follow logs are bounded by ctx), `--` before positional arguments, `--format '{{json .}}'`/inspect JSON instead of text parsing. The child environment uses the fixed allowlist `BaseEnv` with build-specific overrides described in the builds rule; nothing derived from a request may enter it.
- Every created object carries `LabelManaged=true` and `LabelInstance=<instance id>` (`Options.InstanceID`, from `store.InstanceID`; a client without one refuses to work). Listings filter on both and inspect-before-act verifies both (`Client.ours`). Recorded containers are reached through `ServerRef{ServerID, ContainerID}` with the id recorded at creation (`ServerContainer`, `StopServer`, `RemoveServerContainer`, `ServerLogs`, `ServerStats`, `ApplyRestartPolicy`), verified on inspect (`IsServerContainer`; mismatch is `ErrNotOurs`); never by labels alone, since containers inherit image labels. `create` sets every `dev.golemengine.deploy.*` label explicitly (including `build-id` and `engine-version`). `ManagedImage`/`ListImages` require the managed and instance labels (listing and deletion only); a server image is run only after `BuildImage(BuildRef)` verified the full id recorded on a successful build and exactly that build's labels (`BuildRef.Labels`, the same set `BuildSpec.Labels` gave `docker build`; `matchBuildLabels` is shared with `BuiltImage`; `ErrNotManaged`/`ErrNotOurs` otherwise). `docker build` (`BuildSpec`, `Build`, `BuiltImage`, `RemoveBuildOutput` in `build.go`) is covered by the builds rule. Published images are removed by immutable id without force. Unpublished output cleanup checks ownership before removing the build tag. Image/volume removal refuses objects used by any container (`ErrInUse`). Keep tests proving foreign, other-instance and impostor objects get no destructive argv.
- Record a random launch identity before `CreateServer` and the resulting container ID before `StartServer`. Pending-create recovery verifies the explicit launch label, instance, server ID and name before acting. Env values reach only create.
- Env values never appear in argv, the CLI environment, logs or errors: `CreateServer` passes `--env-file` from `envFileFunc` (Linux: pipe as `/dev/fd/3` via `Cmd.ExtraFiles`; elsewhere a 0600 file in a 0700 `tmp/docker-envfile-*` dir, swept by `SweepEnvFiles`). `EncodeEnvFile` relies on `ValidateEnvValue` (UTF-8, no NUL/CR/LF, `MaxEnvVarBytes`) so every line is an unambiguous `KEY=value`. `GOLEM_*` names are reserved for `RunSpec.DaemonEnv`. Docker stderr of `create` is redacted before it lands in `CmdError.Detail`.
- `RunSpec` applies the hardening on every create (exact argv in `client_test.go`): `--pull never`, `--restart no` (the service applies `RestartPolicy` after the settle window), `--init`, `--no-healthcheck`, `--stop-signal SIGTERM`, read-only rootfs, caps dropped, no-new-privileges, pids/memory(+swap)/cpu limits, non-root user, `ImageVolumeTmpfs` on `/tmp` and every image `VOLUME` (`MaxImageVolumes`; daemon mounts win), `rm --force --volumes`. `DaemonEnv` (the engine contract), `ReadOnlyBinds` (the certificate directory; `ValidateBindSource`), `GroupAdd` (never 0), `AdminPort` (published `127.0.0.1::<port>/tcp` unless `AdminHostPort` is set; read back with `Container.LoopbackPort` from inspect `NetworkSettings.Ports`), `BuildID`/`Commit`/`EngineVersion` (labels) and `DataVolume` (the server's data volume at `/data`) are in use; `GID` is for a later milestone. `LabelHelper`, `LabelInspector` (`true` with the Inspector agent variables) and `LabelInspectorHub` (empty) are set explicitly on server containers, and both Inspector labels empty on helpers. Change hardening only with that test and `docs/SERVERS.md`.
- The Inspector hub (`inspector.go`, exact argv in `inspector_test.go`): `HubSpec` has the server hardening, one CPU, no mounts, `--restart unless-stopped`, `GOLEM_INSPECTOR_*` variables only in an env file, port 8787 on `127.0.0.1` only, every label explicit with `inspector-hub=true`, created from the id `ResolveImage` returned (`ValidateHubImageRef`). It is reached only through `InspectorHubs`/`InspectorHub` (`IsInspectorHub`: our labels, the hub label, the fixed name, no server id). `PullImage` runs with `isolatedClientEnv` (the build client configuration: anonymous unless an operator added credentials there, never the operator's own configuration). `ManagedNetworkAddresses` reads the managed network's subnets and gateways. The fake supports `pull` (`AddRegistryImage`, `SetPullHook`) and network IPAM.
- Data volumes and helpers (`data.go`): `CreateDataVolume` labels and then verifies the volume (Docker returns an existing name unchanged); `DataVolume`/`RemoveDataVolume` require managed, instance and the server's id. Helpers (`HelperSpec`, exact argv in `data_test.go`) are created from a verified build image and never started: only the volume mounted (read-only for reads), `--network none`, read-only root, caps dropped, no-new-privileges, uid 65532, pids limit, no ports or env, `--pull never`, a non-existent entrypoint, tmpfs on image volumes, every label explicit plus `helper=<launch id>`, name `golem-deploy_helper_<launch id>`. A helper is reached only through its recorded launch id (`Helper`: recorded container id, or launch label plus name when the id was lost), never by labels alone; `RemoveHelper` reports whether it found one. `CopyFromHelper`/`CopyToHelper` run `docker cp` with `--` and cut the CLI off when the consumer or producer fails (`Cmd.Stdin` carries the tar in). The fake implements `volume create`, helper mounts and `cp` both ways over in-memory volume content (`SetVolumeFiles`, `VolumeFiles`, `SetCopyHook`, `CopiedIn`), copies `Image.Content` into an empty volume on create unless the mount says `volume-nocopy` (like Docker), and moves a dynamically published port with `SetPublishedPort`.
- `EnsureNetwork` creates `golem-deploy` with `NetworkICCOption=false`, recreates our (or a legacy, instance-less) network when it allows ICC and nothing is attached, keeps it with a warning (`isolated=false`) otherwise, and refuses unmanaged (`ErrNotManaged`) or other-instance (`ErrNotOurs`) networks.
- Errors are `*CmdError` wrapping a sentinel (`ErrUnavailable`, `ErrNotFound`, `ErrImageMissing`, `ErrConflict`, `ErrPortBusy`, ...); its `Detail` (CLI stderr) goes to the daemon log only, never to job logs or HTTP responses. `Reachable` caches the `docker version` probe for `GET /status`.
- `internal/ports` validates `--port-range` (1024 ≤ range < 32768, not containing the panel port, browser-unsafe ports skipped), picks the lowest free port and probes the host (TCP+UDP bind). `EphemeralOverlap` reads the host's real ephemeral range (Linux `/proc`) so the daemon can warn at startup.
- The fake (`dockertest.Fake`) reports published ports in inspect, assigns `Image.AssignPort` (e.g. a test admin server) to dynamic publishes, records binds and `--group-add`, exposes `LatestEnv`/`DeleteImage` for service tests, and understands `docker build` (`SetBuildHook`: output, failure, blocking, extra labels, classic or containerd image ids).
- Verify with `go test ./internal/docker/... ./internal/ports/...`; with Docker available also `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DOCKER_TESTS=1 go test -p 1 ./internal/docker/... ./internal/httpapi/...` (builds `gdm2h-*` images from a digest-pinned busybox, uses `dockertest.IntegrationInstanceID`, cleans up its containers and data volumes; `TestIntegrationDataVolumes` checks volume ownership, `docker cp` with never-started helpers, links and FIFOs in the stream; CI does not run it). The Linux pipe path needs a Linux docker CLI to verify for real.
- Docker integration test fixtures select a port available on both TCP and UDP, including on Windows where the reserved ranges can differ.
